    whatLede:
      'For small teams, schools and households that already have, or can rent, one Linux server.',
    cards: [
      ['One server, many people', 'Everyone who signs in with GitHub gets their own Linux account, home directory, credentials and agent daemon. The administrator decides who is admitted; nobody picks a Unix user in the browser.'],
      ['Native tools keep the truth', 'Pi stores its own history, <code>git</code> and <code>gh</code> keep their own logins, and workspaces are ordinary directories under <code>~/src</code>. No workspace registry, no copied history, no vendor database.'],
      ['Work outlives the browser', 'Agents run under your daemon on the server. Steer a running agent, queue the next message, answer its questions or stop it, from a desktop or a phone.'],
      ['The rest of the workbench', 'A browser terminal on the same tmux sessions, a Git worktree per agent, a VS Code editor running as you, and your own connections to GitHub, Gitea, GitLab, Google, Microsoft 365 and Dropbox.'],
      ['Your own model account', 'Each person connects their own provider sign-in through Pi, for example a ChatGPT or Claude subscription, or uses a key the server provides. Nobody shares a personal API key.'],
      ['Nothing phones home', 'No telemetry, no relay, no Open Habitat account between your browser and your server. The interface works in English and Finnish, light and dark.'],
    ],
    compareTitle: 'How it compares',
    compareLede: 'Plenty of tools put coding agents in a window. The difference here is who the server is for.',
    compareAria: 'Comparison table',
    piWebUis: 'Pi web UIs',
    rows: [
      ['Built for', 'several people on one shared server', 'one developer running many agents', 'teams, as a hosted service', 'one person', 'groups running notebooks'],
      ['Separation between people', 'Unix accounts', 'none: every paired device is the same user', 'cloud organisation and task permissions', 'none (no sign-in)', 'Unix accounts or containers'],
      ['Where history lives', 'the agent’s native files', 'native files plus its own journal and scrollback', 'synced to the vendor’s Postgres', 'native files plus the UI’s own stores', 'notebooks on disk'],
      ['Agents', 'Pi', '35+ CLI agents', 'Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Pi', 'Pi', 'not an agent host'],
      ['Shape', 'browser, desktop and phone', 'desktop app, phone app, browser client', 'desktop, browser, phone over a cloud', 'browser', 'browser'],
    ],
    notes: [
      '<b>Choose Orca</b> if you want many agents on your own machine and a polished desktop app today. It supports far more agents than Open Habitat does.',
